    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe change is implemented with minimal structural impact. When two owners share a household (same last name and address), they now return the same non-blank `householdId`, and `sharesHousehold: true` continues to permit creation past the duplicate-household guard.\n\n**Key design decision:** rather than add a persisted `householdId` column to the large `Owner` entity (which the cost metric penalizes heavily \u2014 every new getter/setter would pay the whole class's `WMC_other`), I derive `householdId` as a pure function of the normalized `lastName` + `address`. Two owners in the same household deterministically resolve to the same identifier, with no stored state and no lookup \u2014 a genuinely \"stable shared identifier.\"\n\nThree small, cohesive changes:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `householdId` readOnly string to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n2. **`HouseholdId.java`** (new, single static method) \u2014 SHA-256 over normalized `lastName|address`, rendered as `H-<12 hex>`.\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` expression populating `householdId` on `toOwnerDto`, alongside the existing `displayName`/`initials` expressions.\n\nNo change to the `Owner` entity, repository, DB schema, or the POST pipeline functions. The existing `RejectDuplicateHousehold` guard (which already skips when `sharesHousehold` is true) is untouched.",
